<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Debt does most of its damage while unexamined. Four balances in four
    places, four interest rates nobody has written next to each other, and a
    monthly payment to each that feels like progress and often isn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    Putting them on one page, with the rates visible, changes the arithmetic
    from a feeling into a fact.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What it gives you</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "All of it, in one list") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Balance, rate, minimum payment. The unglamorous act of writing them
        down together is most of the value, and it's the step people avoid
        because of how it feels rather than how hard it is.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "An order to kill them in") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        With the reasoning shown, so you can disagree. Paying the expensive
        one first saves the most money; paying the small one first is
        sometimes what keeps a person going. Both are legitimate and the
        choice is yours.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A date it ends") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The single most motivating number available: when this is over, if
        you keep doing what you're doing. Vague debt is infinite debt.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Payments on the calendar") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Beside everything else, so the month you're stretched is visible
        before it arrives rather than on the day.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">If someone is chasing you</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Being contacted by a collector is a specific situation with a specific
    correct first move, and it's usually to ask them in writing to prove the
    debt is yours — inside a window that starts running immediately. That's
    a letter here, not something you have to know already.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Not advice, and not judgement</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    This isn't financial advice and won't tell you to consolidate or
    refinance. It does the arithmetic you'd do yourself if you had the
    numbers side by side, and it doesn't editorialise about how you got
    here.
  </p>
</section>
